# ALLOWLIST, not a denylist. Everything is excluded by default and re-included
# below. Deliberate: a denylist fails OPEN — a new file or cache directory ships
# into the build context until someone remembers to exclude it. `.semrel` reached
# ~85 MB that way. This form fails CLOSED: anything new is excluded until it is
# explicitly added here, and the build breaks loudly rather than bloating quietly.
#
# Three Dockerfiles build from this same repo-root context, so the list is the
# union of what all three need:
#   docker/Dockerfile          (prod)  — COPY . . in builder, runs `pnpm run build`
#   docker/Dockerfile.local    (dev)   — COPY . ., runs `pnpm dev` (tsx watch src/)
#   telemetry-backend/Dockerfile       — root package.json + telemetry-backend/
#
# Docker applies last-match-wins, so ordering matters: exclude-all, re-include,
# then re-exclude junk nested inside the re-included directories.

# ── Exclude everything ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
*

# ── Application source ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
!src
!telemetry-backend

# ── Manifests and build config ───────────────────────────────────────────────
!package.json
!pnpm-lock.yaml
!pnpm-workspace.yaml
!tsconfig.json
# engine-strict=true — enforces engines.node during the in-image `pnpm install`.
# Excluding this silently drops that guard, so it must stay.
!.npmrc
# Optional: the [d] glob in the Dockerfiles makes it a no-op when absent locally.
!CHANGELOG.md

# ── Re-exclude junk nested inside the re-included directories ────────────────
# `!telemetry-backend` above would otherwise pull in its node_modules and build
# output. Patterns must be re-stated here because the re-include came later.
**/node_modules
**/dist
**/coverage
**/tests
**/*.test.ts
**/vitest.config.ts
**/vitest.setup.ts
# Secrets must never enter the context, at any depth. telemetry-backend/.env
# holds decrypted SOPS values on a developer machine.
**/.env
**/.env.*
**/config.yml
**/*.pem
**/*.key
**/*.cert
**/.DS_Store
